The code statements that lead to the bug are:
kthread_complete_and_exit(&handle->completion, 0);
return kthread_stop(handle->task);
Reason:
The kthread can call `kthread_complete_and_exit` to self-terminate, which frees its `task_struct`. If this happens before the external code calls `kthread_stop(handle->task)`, then `kthread_stop` accesses the already freed kthread object, causing a use-after-free (UAF) due to the race condition.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
wait_for_completion(&handle->completion);
Reason:
Replacing `kthread_stop(handle->task)` with `wait_for_completion(&handle->completion)` eliminates the unsafe external stop. Instead, the code waits for the kthread to complete its own exit via `kthread_complete_and_exit`, which signals the completion. This avoids the race by ensuring the kthread object is only accessed while it is still alive.

